  "query_preview": "..\n925 am Update: With a robust Chinook arch in place over central \nand NE Montana, increased Sky percentages for the remainder of \ntoday and tonight. Not a big deal to most but we have had calls in\nthe past with situations like this.\n\nRemainder of forecast looks to be in great shape. TFJ\n\nPrevious Discussion...\nSynoptic Setup: \n\nForecast begins with zonal flow running from off the coast of the\nPacific Northwest through the PCNW and Montana. North of this is \ncooler air at the surface embedded in a broad trough over southern\nCanada that has leaked down into the Plains and northeast \nMontana. To the south a large closed low sits over the Four \nCorners with a ridge just west of it running north from the Baja \nand cresting over northern California. \n\nToday into tonight: Zonal flow across the region will have weak \nembedded disturbances in the flow that form a modest pressure \ngradient force across the region today. This will lead to breezy \nwest winds in the afternoon which look to be just short of a Lake \nWind Advisory. While it currently doesn't look to be strong enough\nit will need to be monitored for a last minute uptick. In this \nsame vein, downslope wind will generate lower RH across the region\nand if winds were to tick up quite a bit above expected totals \nthen Fire Weather concerns could quickly rear their ugly head as \nwell. Wind speeds and gusts will need to be monitored through the \nday. \n\nWednesday into Friday: As the Four Corners low migrates east onto\nthe Plains, the ridge behind it will widen out along the \nIntermountain West and begin bumping up against the PCNW/MT Zonal\nflow and begin constricting and orienting it to a more NW flow\npattern. The constriction will allow some lift zones to show up\nwhich could lead to little sub-synoptic disturbance for isolated\nchances at showers and thunderstorms as they pass by northeast MT\nduring these times. Though, QPF totals look minimal if anything\nmore than a trace. \n\nFriday night onward: Northwest flow will continue to enhance as\nthe ridge rides up into the PCNW and southern British Columbia. \nThis will make conditions mostly dry into the Weekend. With\nsignificant model breakdown beginning around Sunday/Sunday night \ninto early next work week. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\n- Moderate confidence in winds this afternoon staying below Red \n  Flag or Lake Wind Criteria. \n\n- Low to Moderate confidence in chances for isolated showers\n  Wednesday through Friday.\n\n- Moderate confidence in partial frost Thursday morning.\n\nGAH",
